Security Vulnerability Report: Base44 Agent Can Create and Publicly Upload a Complete Application Archive Through Exposed Platform Capabilities
KBS <admin@dtrh.net>


Summary

I discovered a workflow in which a Base44 application’s AI agent can access the application’s underlying workspace, programmatically construct a complete archive of the project using the Node.js runtime, and upload that archive to Base44’s public file-storage infrastructure using the Core.UploadFile integration.

This appears to circumvent the normal user-facing project export limitation for the account/plan under which I tested it.

The resulting archive is served through a Base44 URL of the form:

https://base44.app/api/apps/{APP_ID}/files/mp/public/{APP_ID}/{REF_ID}_{FILENAME}.gz

In my case, the resulting archive was:

5bfa0cb46_qualia-flowtar.gz

The archive was subsequently recovered in my GitHub repository and contains the project workspace.

I am reporting this because the workflow appears to cross a product security/control boundary: functionality that is not exposed through the normal project-export interface can nevertheless be composed from capabilities available to the AI agent.

I have only tested this against an application and account that I control. I have not attempted to access, enumerate, or retrieve another user’s application or files.

⸻

Potential Impact

The primary concern is that the AI agent appears capable of combining otherwise legitimate capabilities to perform an operation that is normally restricted or unavailable through the product UI:

1. Read the application’s workspace.
2. Access project source files.
3. Generate an arbitrary binary archive.
4. Invoke Base44’s file-upload functionality.
5. Place the resulting archive into Base44’s public file-serving infrastructure.
6. Return a publicly addressable file URL.

If the inability to export the complete application is intended to be a plan-level or authorization boundary, this workflow may allow that boundary to be bypassed.

There is also a potentially more significant security concern if the generated public file URL is accessible to parties who do not otherwise have authorization to access the application’s source or workspace contents.

I have intentionally not tested access to another user’s application or attempted to enumerate identifiers.

⸻

Affected Components

The observed workflow involves the following Base44 capabilities:

* AI Agent / application workspace execution environment
* Application filesystem/workspace
* Node.js runtime
* Core.UploadFile integration
* Base44 public file-serving infrastructure
* /api/apps/{APP_ID}/files/mp/public/... file endpoint

Base44’s documentation describes Core.UploadFile as an integration for uploading files to public storage and obtaining a file URL.

⸻

Technical Details

The workflow begins inside the Base44 application workspace.

The agent was able to establish that the application workspace was accessible through /app.

Instead of relying on an operating-system tar executable or an external archiving package, the archive was constructed directly in Node.js.

The implementation used functionality equivalent to:

const fs = require("fs");
const path = require("path");
const zlib = require("zlib");

The TAR archive was constructed programmatically using Node.js Buffer objects. TAR headers were generated directly, including the USTAR header:

const header = Buffer.alloc(512, 0);
header.write("ustar\0", 257, "ascii");

The resulting TAR byte stream was then compressed using Node’s built-in gzip functionality.

Conceptually:

/app
  ↓
recursive filesystem traversal
  ↓
TAR byte stream
  ↓
zlib gzip
  ↓
.gz archive

This means an external tar binary is not required.

⸻

Upload Mechanism

The resulting binary was then passed to Base44’s built-in file-upload integration.

The relevant operation was conceptually:

await base44.integrations.Core.UploadFile({
    file: archive
});

Because the execution environment is Node.js while the upload interface can operate with browser-compatible file objects, the archive can be represented as a Blob where necessary:

const blob = new Blob([gzBuffer], {
    type: "application/gzip"
});

The upload operation returned a Base44-hosted file URL.

The resulting URL followed this structure:

https://base44.app/api/apps/{APP_ID}/files/mp/public/{APP_ID}/{REF_ID}_{FILENAME}.gz

The URL subsequently resolves through Base44’s public media infrastructure.

⸻

Observed Artifact

The resulting archive was:

5bfa0cb46_qualia-flowtar.gz

It was approximately 135 KB.

The archive was subsequently committed to my GitHub repository as part of the recovered project:

https://github.com/DTRHnet/markov-qualia-consciousness-simulator

The archive is present in the repository root and was included in the repository’s initial snapshot.

The archive filename provides an additional indication that the Base44-generated URL contains two distinct identifiers:

5bfa0cb46_qualia-flowtar.gz
^^^^^^^^^
   REF_ID

The application identifier appears separately in the URL path:

/api/apps/{APP_ID}/files/mp/public/{APP_ID}/

Therefore, my current understanding is:

APP_ID
    ↓
identifies the Base44 application
REF_ID
    ↓
identifies the uploaded file/object
FILENAME
    ↓
qualia-flowtar

I initially referred to the application identifier as sess_id in my notes, but after comparing the generated application code and Base44’s URL structure, I believe app_id is the more accurate interpretation.

